= Naming and Necessity =
[: [[local:zz.texts/kripke1980-namingAndNecessity.pdf]], https://n2t.net/ark:/13960/t40s0jk5g :]

====== short ======
Perhaps the most influential exposition of the new modal actualism.

====== related to ======
??lewis-counterfactuals??,
[[identity_of_indiscernables]]

[[local:zz.texts/loomis1999-necessityAPrioriStandardMeter.pdf]]

Soames, 2002, _Beyond Rigidity: The Unfinished Semantic Agenda of Naming and Necessity_

= observations =
== rigid designation akin to scope ==
The whole rigid designator issue is a kind of scope error.

I = the person who utters this statement = ∃x x utters this statement . . . 

Of course the “this” is still indexical. It may be that the ancients were
wrong and indexicals are ineliminable. But the relationship that holds between
the indexical term (token) itself and the object it refers to can be.
If we think of the scope of that relationship as being inside or outside the
scope of the quantifiers in the (analysed) statement itself. Rigid is tight
scope, non-rigid is wide scope.

== theses / foundations ==
=== the importance of identity ===
It seems the fundamental principle that
see end of next section for Quinean version of the requirement of identity and
Kripke's disruption of the fundamental computational/inferential role of
identity (substitution always guarantees truth).

= notes on first reading by section =
== preface ==
    The model theory made this completely clear, though it should have been
    clear enough on the intuitive level. Waiving fussy considerations deriving
    from the fact that x need not have necessary existence, it was clear from
    (x) ☐(x = x) and Leibnitz's law that identity is an 'internal' relation:
    (x) (y) (x = y => ☐ x = y). (What pairs (x, y) could be counterexamples?
    Not pairs of distinct objects, for then the antecedent is false; nor any
    pair of an object and itself, for then the consequent is true.) If 'a' and
    'b' are rigid designators, it follows that 'a = b', if true, is a necessary
    truth. If 'a' and 'b' are not rigid designators, no such conclusion
    follows about the statement 'a = b' (though the objects designated by 'a'
    and 'b' will be necessarily identical).
The sense of “Leibnitz's law” that Kripke relies upon here is the less
controversial (but less clearly said by Leibniz) indiscernability of identicals
(see [[identity_of_indiscernables#Leibnizs law]]):
|----------------------------------|
| (x)(y)( x = y =>. F(x) => F(y) ) |
In this case the open formula in place of F(...) is: ☐ ( x = ... ) producing:
|------------------------------------------|
| (x)(y)( x = y =>. ☐(x = x) => ☐(x = y) ) |

from that and (z)☐(z = z) it may be inferred that
|-----------------------------|
| (x)(y)( x = y => ☐(x = y) ) |

This pattern of inference involves
two points:
=== internal relations - all or none ?? ===
At first glance this appears to be a beautiful explication of internal
relations. It is at least prima facie appealing that a relation R is internal
iff:
    (x) (y) ( x R y ⊃ ☐ x R y )
But what gives this the sense of substance is the universal quantification of x
and y.  Does it really have to be the case that if a relationship is internal
for one pair of objects then it is internal for all the pairs of objects for
which it obtains? (And likewise for properties, if it is internal for one
object/type of object must be be internal for all??). That is the claim made in
that Kripke's suggested explication of “internal property”.

However, consider the table in my kitchen and the table in my
living room. I presume that it is not necessarily true that my kitchen table is
longer than my living room table. If I dub my kitchen table “Alia” and my
living room table “Beethoven” so that they are “rigidly designated” by
these names then I presume (sadly I seem to lack the relevant modal intuitions)
that it is not necessarily the case that Alia is longer than Beethoven. Hence
the relationship of being longer than is not an internal relationship by
Kripke's definition.

But, to build upon an example Kripke presents in [[#lecture 1]], consider
the relationship between 2 metres and 1 metre (if you are uncomfortable reifying
meters, me too, but it is Kripke's example). Surely it is necessarily the case
that the length rigidly designated (according to Kripke) by “2 meters” is
longer than that designated by “1 meter”. Does this make [longer_than] an
internal relationship for meter lengths even though it is not internal for every
kind of object?

A natural suggestion is to relativise internal relationships to types of
object.  Relation R is internal for objects of type T iff:
    (x)(y) (T(x) ∧ T(y) ∧ x R y .⊃ ☐ x R y)
In this manner we may recognise [longer_than] as an internal relationship
for lengths, but not for tables.  But this won't seem to do:

=== weirdness with non-rigid designators ===
Lets focus on the last sentence:
    If 'a' and 'b' are not rigid designators, no such conclusion follows about
    the statement 'a = b' (though the objects designated by 'a' and 'b' will
    be necessarily identical).
So lets say that Ali is the only red car on my street. Then “Ali” rigidly
designates Ali and the definite description “the red car on my street”
non-rigidly designates Ali. So too if Ali is the only Holden on my street then
the definite description “the Holden on my street” also non-rigidly
designates Ali.

Now it is not true that “Necessarily the red car on my street is the Holden on
my street” but it is true that “Necessarily, the object designated by 'the
red car on my street' is identical to the object designated by 'the Holden on
my street'”. So within the scope of a ☐ replacing a denoting phrase “N”
with “the object referred to by the phrase 'N'” does not always preserve
truth.

More generally “☐ P(the object referred to by 'Name')” may be true and yet
“☐ P(Name)” be false. (We don't have to consider P as just a predicate
here, but as any closed formula.) It is, apparently, true by Kripke's standards
(given his later claims that objects have their origins essentially) that
“Necessarily the object designated by 'the red car on my street' was built in
China.” but false that “Necessarily the red car on my street was built in
China.”

This does not break the principle that substituting any name for both
instances of 'N' in “'N' denotes N” produces a truth. (A principle that
seems seems every bit as non-negotiable as “'P' is true iff P”.) But we do
have a startling failure of substitutivity that comes close – a strange breach
of referential disquotation.

Naively one would have thought that quotation
marks around a term (name or definite description) and “the object denoted by
by ...”, would express kind of inverse operations. Just as quotation marks
applied to sentences and “is true”. When one applies “is true” to a
quoted sentence one gets another that is true just in case the quoted original
is true.  Indeed this is what (I thought) the disquotational theory of truth was
all about.  (See [[disquotational_truth]]) But this

This is a high price to pay. For non rigid designators within the scope
of necessity operators substitutivity fails. Breaking the complexity of the
underlying logic.

If substitutivity fails _in this case_, if one can't even substitute “the object
designated by 'P'” for 'P', without changing truths to falsehoods then there
is surely no hope for some criteria for transworld identity (which would amount
to a standard for new transworld entities). We could tell when two terms refer
to the same entity, and when they do not.

Viewed from the point of view of computational complexity


== lecture 1 ==
[[influential_semantic_theories#Kripkes modal argument ]]
Phase 1 of this lecture sets about the task of refreshing readers' memory of
Fregean/Russellian accounts of (ordinary) names as (analysable into) definite
descriptions. (Ordinary names of objects that are not, in Russell's terminology,
known by acquaintance.) Then this is widened to include more somewhat more
complex (and perhaps more vague, though Kripke doesn't bring that into the
picture) then a widening of this account into clusters or families.

At this point Kripke somewhat carelessly mixes two different accounts of names -
describing both as “What we really associate with the name is a family of
descriptions.” But takes this as describing both
	1) Frege's account that “In the case of genuinely proper names like
	   'Aristotle' opinions as regards their sense may diverge.” According to
	   which different speakers use the name “Aristotle” to express
	   different senses – but “As long as the nominatum remains the same,
	   these fluctuations in sense are tolerable”. I.e. each competent
	   speaker uses 'Aristotle' in a different sense, but so long as those
	   diverse senses are (uniquely?) true of the same person (i.e. Aristotle)
	   uses each counts as using the name correctly.
AND
	2) The more or less Wittgensteinian view that the name expresses a kind of
	   weighted cluster or family of descriptions. Few or none of them _must_ be
	   true of the object referred to by the relevant name. But a sufficient
	   collection of them is required to be true of the named object (otherwise
	   the named object does not exist/the name fails to refer).

Kripke *does* justify the shift to (2) on the grounds that “even a single
speaker when asked 'What description are you willing to substitute for the
name?' may be quite at a loss. In fact, he may know many things about him; but
any particular thing that he knows he may feel clearly expresses a contingent
property of the object”. But his way of shifting from one to the other may
mislead a less than scrupulous reader into confusing the two. He describes them
both as ways of explaining how names don't express a simple shared definite
description. (But Frege gives up shared, while Wittgenstein gives up simple.)
Note: :my-thesis: of :non-linguistic-representation: solves this problem.

Then he distinguishes between these (clusters of)

